<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Bahia</h1><p class="st-meta">1534 CE – 1821 CE · capital Salvador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The captaincy around the Bay of All Saints that became the seat of colonial Brazil's government and the heart of its sugar and slave economy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">King John III</a> granted the Captaincy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-all-saints">Bay of All Saints</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pereira-coutinho">Francisco Pereira Coutinho</a>. His settlement failed, and he was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> in 1547. The crown bought back the captaincy, and in 1549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> there as the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> governors-general and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> until 1763 and the first bishopric, from 1551. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconcavo">Recôncavo</a> around the bay was filled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations and tobacco farms, and Salvador was one of the main ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> a strong African culture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> briefly took Salvador in 1624 and 1625.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1763. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>, though Portuguese troops held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> until 2 July 1823, still celebrated as Bahia's independence day.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-of-bahia.jpg" alt="Captaincy of Bahia" width="320" height="424"><figcaption>Image: Tonyjeff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_of_Bahia_(ancient).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1534 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salvador</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Pereira Coutinho, Tomé de Sousa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Captaincy of the Bay of All Saints, Bahia de Todos os Santos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=captaincy-of-bahia" aria-label="Captaincy of Bahia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3624 2661 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Bahia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3944 2917L3944 2917M3944 2917L3936 3045M3944 2917L3933 2984"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3936 3045h0M3933 2984h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3944 2917h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salvador. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=captaincy-of-bahia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
